From 90b864f9c91b2312a63d63f3b9ac87beedc03a68 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michal Szpakowski <michi.szpakowski@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 20:09:22 +0200
Subject: [RFC PATCH 3/3] ALSA: hda/cs8409: Add support for Apple
 MacBookPro13,1

The 2016 13" MacBook Pro without Touch Bar has a CS8409 with codec
subsystem ID 106b:3300, a CS42L83 for the headset jack and four SSM3515
speaker amplifiers, all on the I2C bridge of the CS8409. Add it as a new
board next to the Dell ones.

Layout: the speakers are on ASP1 (pins 0x24/0x25, converters 0x02/0x03),
the amplifiers at I2C 0x14-0x17, one TDM slot each. The CS42L83 is on
ASP2 (headphone pin 0x2c from converter 0x0a, microphone pin 0x3c to
ADC 0x1a). The internal microphone is DMIC1. GPIO 0 is the CS42L83
interrupt, GPIO 1 its reset.

The register values come from the out-of-tree snd_hda_macbookpro driver
(https://github.com/davidjo/snd_hda_macbookpro), which replays the
sequences of AppleHDA, read down to the register level and put into the
structure of this driver: the vendor coefficient table, the CS42L83 init
sequence with the serial port and sample rate converter settings
AppleHDA uses for 44.1 kHz, the SSM3515 setup. Only 44.1 kHz is
supported, as nothing else is known. Not taken over, with nothing found
to depend on them: the CS42L83 PLL, which AppleHDA programs and starts
but, with MCLK_SRC_SEL left at 0, does not use; three CS42L83 registers
outside cs42l42.h (0x107e, 0x1b08, 0x2102); the vendor verb 0x7f0 and
the writes to coefficients 0x6b, 0x6c and 0x71. The amplifier slot map
is the out-of-tree driver's, not the one in AppleHDA's log; left and
right were checked by ear, which amplifier drives which driver of a
side is not known.

What differs from the Dell boards:

- The fixup is also picked by DMI. The codec subsystem ID the firmware
  writes at boot is no longer there after the controller has been probed
  again, for instance after a module reload; the CS8409 then reports its
  own ID. The quirk matches the subsystem ID, which other models may
  share (the out-of-tree driver uses it for the MacBookPro14,1 too; not
  tested); the DMI entry names the 13,1 only.

- The CS42L83 is brought up around each stream instead of once at init:
  when a stream is prepared, the blocks of its direction are powered,
  the chip is switched from its oscillator to the bit clock of ASP2, and
  only then are the serial port channels enabled; the last cleanup takes
  it back. The ASoC cs42l42 driver and AppleHDA work per stream as well.
  Like AppleHDA, and unlike the ASoC driver, this enables the channels
  after the clock switch: enabled before it, the receive side now and
  then stayed deaf for a whole stream. Unlike AppleHDA, which powers the
  blocks after enabling the channels and the headphone amplifier last,
  the blocks are powered first, all at once. Put on the bit clock once
  from the init with everything up, the Dell way, the headphone path
  came up right for one stream and distorted or silent for the next.
  Every stream goes through this, also one that only uses the speakers
  or the internal microphone. Before a system suspend powers the chip
  down under a running stream, where no cleanup has run, it is taken
  back to its oscillator too.

- Runtime suspend does not reset the CS42L83. It stays out of reset,
  powered down and on its oscillator, with its tip sense interrupt and
  the GPIO unsolicited response left enabled, so a plug or unplug wakes
  the codec; the resume reads the tip sense status and acts on a change.
  A headset that stays plugged is thus not detected again on every
  resume: that detection ran under each stream started from idle, with
  the speakers on until it was done. This light way out is taken only
  for a runtime resume that finds the CS8409 as it was left; every
  system sleep is followed by the full init, which, as on the Dell
  boards, starts from "no jack": a headset left in is reported out and
  then in again. (The core leaves power_state at RESUME after a system
  suspend aborted before the codec's callback ran; until the next resume
  the board then behaves as after a system sleep.) A shutdown runs as a
  runtime suspend and leaves the chip in standby. The jack handler holds
  a runtime PM reference while it runs, so that it cannot work into a
  runtime suspend; an interrupt that finds the codec not active asks for
  a resume instead. A system suspend takes the jack lock.

- The common suspend function gets a per-board hook, spec->suspend, and
  the sync work below another, spec->irq_sync.

- An interrupt raised before the card is registered is recovered. The
  HDA core drops unsolicited responses until then, the jack detection
  run from the build action raises its interrupt before, nobody reads
  the CS42L83 status and the line stays low: no later event makes an
  edge. A delayed work looks at the line once responses get through.
  The Dell boards run the same detection at the same point; whether to
  solve this for all boards is a question for the list.

- The tip sense gets a 1 s rise debounce (the Dell tables have none, the
  ASoC driver defaults to 1 s) and sets the flag of the previous patch.
  Without the debounce the type detection ran while the plug was still
  sliding in and took a headset for headphones on 4 of about 19 plugs
  under a running stream; with it, on none of 33, with a stream running
  or with the codec runtime suspended.

- The speakers are one stereo pair on two converters: the PCM is limited
  to two channels and the second converter gets a copy of the first.
  They get a "Speaker Playback Volume" on the digital volume of the
  amplifiers. It stops at the -3 dB AppleHDA programs: there is no
  speaker protection here, and nothing in this driver knows what the
  drivers stand above that level.

- The jack handler, the PCM hooks and the speaker volume are serialised
  by a mutex of their own. The runtime PM callbacks do not need it: the
  handler is kept off them by its runtime PM reference.

Tested on a MacBookPro13,1 with this driver built as a module against
Fedora's 7.2.8: speakers, speaker volume, headphones and headset
microphone (Koss Porta Pro with microphone), internal microphone, plug
and unplug with a stream running and with the codec runtime suspended,
playback started from idle, S3 (deep) from idle and under a running
stream, hibernation with the speakers in use and with a headset plugged
in.

Not tested: s2idle. Not handled: headset buttons; sample rates other
than 44.1 kHz. The CS8409's PLLs and serial ports stay enabled while the
codec is in D3.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
